hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1397","\nNarrated to us Muhammad ibn Yahya, narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, who narrated to me Thalabah ibn 'Abbad al-'Abdiy from the people of al-Basrah, that he witnessed a sermon one day delivered by Samurah ibn Jundub. In his sermon, Samurah ibn Jundub (RA) said:\n\n\One day"","" while I was with a young Ansari man"","" we were shooting an arrow at our target during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). When the sun was about two or three spear-lengths above the horizon—though not in the direction where it sets—it suddenly turned black"","" as if it were a piece of dark cloth. One of us said to the other"","" 'Let us go to the mosque"","" for by Allah"","" this unusual phenomenon of the sun must mean that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) will inform his nation of something significant.'\n\nSo we hurried to the mosque and found the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) already outside"","" having come out to address the people. We arrived just as he was about to step forward. He led us in prayer"","" standing in the first rak'ah for the longest time he had ever stood in any prayer with us"","" yet we did not hear a single word from him. Then he bowed for the longest time he had ever bowed in any prayer with us"","" yet we did not hear a single word from him. Then he prostrated for the longest time he had ever prostrated in any prayer with us"","" yet we did not hear a single word from him.\n\nHe did the same in the second rak'ah. The sun's eclipse ended just as he sat in the second rak'ah. Then he gave the salutation"","" praised Allah"","" and extolled Him. He bore witness that there is no god but Allah and that he is His slave and Messenger. Then he said:\n\n'O people! I am only a human being"","" the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). I remind you of Allah—if you know that I have fallen short in conveying any of the messages of my Lord"","" then you should have asked me until I conveyed them to you as they ought to be conveyed. But if you know that I have conveyed the messages of my Lord"","" then you should have informed me.'\n\nThe people stood up and said"","" 'We bear witness that you have conveyed the messages of your Lord"","" advised your nation"","" and fulfilled what was upon you.'\n\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) then remained silent for a while. Then he said:\n\n'As for what follows: Indeed"","" some people claim that the eclipse of this sun"","" the eclipse of this moon"","" and the disappearance of these stars from their positions are due to the death of great men on earth. They have lied! Rather"","" these are signs from the signs of Allah"","" by which He tests His servants"","" so that He may see who among them will repent. By Allah"","" since I stood to pray"","" I have seen what you will face in your worldly life and the Hereafter.\n\nBy Allah"","" the Hour will not be established until thirty liars emerge"","" the last of whom will be the Dajjal (Antichrist)"","" who is one-eyed"","" with his left eye smeared"","" resembling the eye of Abu Yahya or the eye of a certain Ansari elder. When he emerges"","" he will claim to be Allah. Whoever believes in him"","" confirms him"","" and follows him will not benefit from any righteous deed he has done. Whoever disbelieves in him and denies him will not be punished for any of his past deeds.\n\nHe will appear over the entire earth except for the Haram (Sacred Mosque in Makkah) and Bayt al-Maqdis (Jerusalem). He will come to the believers in Bayt al-Maqdis"","" and they will be severely shaken. Allah will then defeat him and his armies until even the trunks of walls and the roots of trees will call out"","" saying"","" 'O believer! This disbeliever is hiding behind me. Come and kill him!'\n\nThis will not happen until you see matters that distress you so greatly that you will ask one another whether your Prophet (ﷺ) mentioned anything about them. It will not happen until mountains are uprooted from their places after this event"",""' and he pointed with his hand.\""\n\nAbu Bakr (a narrator in the chain) said: \""This wording in this report—that 'we did not hear a single word from him'—is of a type that we are not accustomed to accept"","" for the report must be from someone who informs of the existence of something"","" not from someone who denies it. Aishah (RA) has reported that the Prophet (ﷺ) recited aloud in prayer"","" so her report must be accepted"","" for she preserved the fact that he recited aloud"","" even if others did not preserve it. It is possible that Samurah was in a distant row from the Prophet (ﷺ) during the recitation"","" so his statement 'we did not hear a single word from him' means 'I did not hear a voice"",""' as I have explained earlier—that the Arabs say 'there was not such-and-such' when it is unknown whether it existed.\""\n\n---"""""
